few farmers can remember a harvest as  dry and dusty as this it's an anxious  time not just because harvesting on  flinty soil can bring the risk of sparks  and fire  this drought is already impacting yields  of certain crops and adding extra costs  to feeding livestock  so after months of dry weather and now  the really hot temperature you you can  see the grass is brown there's no food  left out for the animals in the field so  we're having to feed  next year next winter's food already but  many farmers are now prevented from  accessing the water they desperately  need now that drought has been declared  in many regions  given the future being mapped out for us  by climate change it's time for action  they argue we have enough water in this  country it's normally in the west and in  the winter and we need investment in  infrastructure to have it in the right  place at the right time so there's  enough water for people food and the  environment this will not be the last  drought the government need to address  this they are the ones have the overall  control the environment agency clearly  have a major role to play but government  need to lead  today the labour party have called for  an urgent cobra meeting in response to  the crisis  and called for the publication of the  national resilience strategy almost a  year after the consultation closed it's  hard to overestimate the impact this  drought could have if it does drive up  food prices at a time of high inflation  and a cost of living crisis but without  enough water to go around prioritizing  it for agriculture comes at a direct  cost to our environment  and that isn't the only concern this  weekend's high temperatures have bought  more fires this is bag shot in surrey  and it's emerged that despite widespread  warnings about their use this fire in  studland dorset yesterday was caused by  a barbecue according to the local fire  service i don't think i've ever seen the  conditions be so perfect for wildfires  